const description = "What an RCIC is, how to check a licence on the CICC public register, RCIC vs lawyer, what a service agreement must include, red flags and how to complain.";
const canonical = "https://bcpnpcalculator.ca/articles/rcic-regulated-canadian-immigration-consultant";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What does RCIC mean?",
    a: "RCIC stands for Regulated Canadian Immigration Consultant. It is the title used by immigration consultants licensed by the College of Immigration and Citizenship Consultants (CICC), the federal regulator created under the College of Immigration and Citizenship Consultants Act. An RCIC can charge a fee to advise you on, or represent you in, Canadian immigration and citizenship matters.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I verify an RCIC licence?",
    a: "Search the consultant's name or College ID on the CICC public register at register.college-ic.ca. Check that the status is Active (or Active - Practice Restricted, with the restriction listed) and that the Entitled to Practise column says Yes. Then contact the consultant using the phone number or email shown on the register, not only the details on their website or social media.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is an RCIC or an immigration lawyer better?",
    a: "Neither is automatically better. Both are authorized by IRCC to charge for immigration advice and representation. Lawyers can also handle Federal Court judicial reviews and broader legal problems such as criminal or family law issues that affect your immigration case. For routine applications, many people use either, or no representative at all. Compare experience with your specific program, the written fee estimate and the person's licence status.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can an RCIC represent me at the Immigration and Refugee Board?",
    a: "Only if they hold the right licence. The IRB says that since July 1, 2023, CICC members need a specific type of licence to represent clients before the Board, even if they are not charging a fee. On the CICC register this is the Class L3 RCIC-IRB licence. Class L1 and L2 RCICs cannot appear before the IRB unless they are also paralegals licensed by the Law Society of Ontario.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need an RCIC to apply for the BC PNP or Express Entry?",
    a: "No. IRCC says you don't need to hire a representative and that using one will not draw special attention to your application. WelcomeBC says hiring a representative is not required to apply to the BC PNP and does not guarantee approval. All forms and instructions are free on canada.ca and welcomebc.ca.",
  },
  {
    q: "How much does an RCIC charge?",
    a: "There is no official fee schedule. Fees vary by consultant, program and complexity, so ask for a written estimate. The Code of Professional Conduct requires the service agreement to state either the hourly rate and anticipated hours or an agreed fixed fee, plus expected disbursements, taxes, payment terms and the refund policy. Government fees, such as IRCC processing fees, are separate.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I complain about an immigration consultant?",
    a: "For a licensed RCIC, file a complaint through the CICC College Portal; the College does not accept complaints by email, paper or phone. For someone who is not licensed, submit an unauthorized practitioner report through the same portal, and WelcomeBC also suggests reporting unlicensed representatives to local police. If a BC PNP application is involved, email PNP.fraud.tips@gov.bc.ca as well.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
